  "solution": {
    "text": "The electronic configuration of Mg is<br /><math><mn>1</mn><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\"></mi><mn>2</mn><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\"></mi><mn>2</mn><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\"></mi><mn>3</mn><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\"></mi><mn>3</mn><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup></math><br />In it 3s – orbital is completely filed and 3p-orbital is empty. There is very small energy difference between 3s and 3p – orbitals. Thus, they overlap and make the Mg a conductor.<br /><img src=\"assets\\st0063_8e7b9c4578ea.jpeg\" style=\"max-width:100%; object-fit:contain\" style=\"width:3.025in;height:0.94167in\" /><br />Band model of Mg"
